why 8 1/2 LEADING_TONE The alto's leading tone D♯ drops to B, the fifth of the last chord, so that chord has all its notes. The soprano still ends on the tonic.
why 1 0 SIMILAR_MOTION The upbeat rises into the first downbeat: the tune steps up while the bass leaps an octave within the E chord. The harmony stays the same, so nothing is lost.
why 2 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano (A–G♯) and tenor (F♯–G♯) meet on G♯ by step. The tenor moves on at once to A and B.
why 4 0 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Soprano and alto fall D♯–C♯ and G♯–F♯ a fifth apart, but the soprano moves an eighth earlier, so the fifths are staggered. Bach accepts that; in exercises they still count as parallel fifths.
why 6 1/4 PROGRESSION The bass climbs F♯–G♯–A♯–B to the dominant at the fermata. I⁶ is a passing chord on that climb, not a step backward.
why 6 1/2 DOUBLING At the fermata alto (E–D♯) and tenor (C♯–D♯) both reach D♯ by step. It is a half cadence, so the leading tone need not resolve here, and the fifth F♯ is in the soprano.
why 7 0 SIMILAR_MOTION Every voice falls into IV⁶ as the new phrase heads down; the bass's leap G♯–C♯ gives it energy.
why 8 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION In the final cadence every voice falls: F♯–E in the soprano, the alto's D♯ to B, the seventh A to G♯ and the bass to E.
